# Kconfig — Ambiq Apollo510 EVB board options
#
# Source this from your application Kconfig with:
#   rsource "../tapestry-os/boards/apollo510_evb/Kconfig"

config TAPESTRY_AP510_LED_WALK
	bool "Boot-time LED identification walk"
	default n
	help
	  Lights led0, then led1, then led2 in turn for 1.5 s each during
	  substrate_init(), logging the alias and SoC pin of each as it goes.

	  Bring-up aid: the EVB silkscreen numbers its LEDs independently of
	  Zephyr's devicetree alias numbering, and the board carries indicator
	  LEDs (power, J-Link) that are not under software control at all — so
	  "which physical LED is led2" cannot be answered from the board files.
	  This walk answers it by inspection.

	  Adds ~4.5 s to boot and holds off gossip for that long.  Leave off in
	  normal builds.

config TAPESTRY_AP510_PIN_FINDER
	bool "Header pin finder (identify EVB header pins by grounding them)"
	default n
	help
	  Arms every UART1/2/3 TX or RX pin as a pull-up input and reports on
	  the console which SoC pin is grounded, so header positions can be
	  mapped to GPIO numbers without the EVB schematic.

	  Ground a header pin through a ~1 kOhm series resistor and the console
	  prints e.g. "PIN FINDER: P23 GROUNDED".  The resistor matters: it
	  turns an accidental touch on a power rail into a few milliamps
	  instead of a short.

	  P53 (mspi1 chip enable) and P55 (UART0 console RX) are excluded
	  because an enabled peripheral owns them.

	  The element does NOT come up in this mode — substrate_init() enters
	  the finder loop and never returns.  Bring-up only.
